# umount.pdxfs — caps.decl
#
# Format: R90-XREPO.013.M1-001 line-oriented caps.decl grammar
# (paideia-os design/architecture/caps-decl-format.md). Replaces this
# repo's prior R53-vintage prose caps.decl (consumed_kinds narrative
# style) per the R90-XREPO.013.M4-001 adoption wave
# (paideia-os design/user/fs-tools-caps.md §2.3, paideia-os#2131).
#
# umount.pdxfs is a standalone CLI tool (a ring-3 ELF with its own
# `_start`), not a shared library: it performs its own syscalls
# directly rather than delegating every kernel touch to a linked
# library. See src/target_resolve.pdx, src/handle_check.pdx,
# src/unmount_op.pdx, src/unmount_record.pdx for the call sites each
# declared kind below backs.

!KIND_USER                0x001   # invoker attribution (audit; unmount_record.pdx)
!KIND_PDXFS_MOUNT_TABLE   0x006   # READ_ROW + LIST (target_resolve.pdx mount-table walk; row-remove op still unlanded)
 KIND_VOLUME              0x00F   # flush+unmount (rights band, ops TBD; unmount_op.pdx steps 2-4)
 KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT        0x001   # semantic-pipe stdout (pipe_wire.pdx; svc.schema-registry not yet live, deferred)

# Notes:
#
# - KIND_USER and KIND_PDXFS_MOUNT_TABLE are mandatory: umount must
#   attribute audit (KIND_USER) and must be able to walk the mount
#   table to resolve a `<mount-id | mount-point>` positional to a
#   mount_id (KIND_PDXFS_MOUNT_TABLE, target_resolve.pdx). A parent
#   holding less than the declared rights on either fails the
#   exec-time reconcile with -EACCES per the mandatory-narrower-than-
#   decl rule (format spec §4/§5) rather than let this tool do partial
#   work. The mount-table op catalog (`PMT_OP_MAX` = 3: LIST/READ_ROW/
#   APPEND_ROW/QUERY_SLOT_COUNT) still has no REMOVE_ROW ordinal —
#   src/unmount_op.pdx's own row-removal step (§5.5 step 5) is a
#   documented stub (`UO_STUB_ROW_REMOVE`) — the decl reserves the
#   rights band (0x006) so the eventual op lands without a decl
#   amendment.
#
# - KIND_VOLUME is optional: a `--dry-run` umount (target resolution
#   only) never opens the volume; the real flush+unmount path
#   (unmount_op.pdx's six-step pipeline, steps 2-4) does. The narrow
#   keeps this cap out of dry-run child processes. The rights band
#   (0x00F) mirrors this repo's own R53 prose decl's "flush+unmount"
#   label; the KIND's REAL landed op catalog is VOL_OP_QUERY_* +
#   VOL_OP_DEBUG_PRINT only (ordinals 0..9) — there is still no landed
#   "unmount" op, so this reserves the band ahead of the op existing,
#   same posture as mount.pdxfs's own KIND_VOLUME entry.
#
# - KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL is deliberately ABSENT from this decl.
#   `--force` triggers an on-demand elevate-broker request
#   (src/elevate.pdx) per §5.2/§8.2 of design/tooling/volume-tooling-
#   ux.md (paideia-os repo) and R90-XREPO.011 policy — the elevate cap
#   is never held ambiently at exec time, matching the `rm`/
#   mount.pdxfs pattern the round plan pins. (src/elevate.pdx's
#   `Elevate::elevate_request_force_unmount` remains a fail-closed
#   stub predating this decl format — that binding gap is tracked
#   separately and does not change this exec-time declaration.)
#
#   LE-001 (#22) adds a SECOND on-demand elevate case, conditional on
#   the unmount TARGET rather than on `--force`: any unmount whose raw
#   argv string classifies as `/system` (target_resolve.pdx's
#   `target_is_system`) requests elevation via `Elevate::elevate_
#   request_system_unmount` (src/elevate.pdx) BEFORE the mount-table
#   mutation, regardless of --force/--lazy/handle_count. Same posture
#   as the --force case above: on-demand only, never ambient, still a
#   fail-closed stub (no broker-endpoint cap provisioned to this tool)
#   — this decl entry does not change. A non-/system unmount (the
#   common case) never reaches this code path at all.
#
# - KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT is optional: the semantic-pipe stdout binding
#   (src/pipe_wire.pdx) is a real call site but presently inert
#   (svc.schema-registry does not exist yet, see paideia-os#2000), so
#   the line-based sys_write(1, ...) rendering runs with or without
#   this cap. Reserving the decl entry now keeps the eventual real
#   bind a rights-narrow, not a decl amendment.
#
# - This decl is already a signed input in release/manifest.pdxsig.txt
#   [artifacts.source] (`caps.decl blake3-256 = <BLAKE3-caps.decl>`);
#   tampering with a shipped decl fails signature verification before
#   exec ever reaches the reconciler. No manifest change was needed
#   for this adoption pass.
#
# References:
#   paideia-os design/architecture/caps-decl-format.md — format spec (R90-XREPO.013.M1-001)
#   paideia-os design/user/fs-tools-caps.md §2.3        — declared cap-set for this tool
#   paideia-os src/kernel/core/cap/reconcile.pdx        — kernel substrate
#   paideia-os#2131                                     — fs-tools adoption inventory
